|passage=As thus all gazed on hir, so she glaunced hir lookes on all, surueying them as curiously, as they noted hir exactly, but at last she set downe her '''period''' on the face of ''Alexis'' {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|III|1|text=And if my death might make this island happy,<br>And prove the '''period''' of their tyranny,<br>I would expend it with all willingness:}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|III|iii|58|1|Why now let me die, for I haue liu'd long enough : This is the '''period''' of my ambition : O this bleſſed houre.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|II|3|203|passage=All comes to one '''period''', whether man make an end of himſelfe, or whether he endure-it{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1629|author={{w|Sir John Beaumont, 1st Baronet|John Beaumont}}|chapter=A Description of Love|title=Bosworth-field with a Taste of the Variety of Other Poems|location=London|publisher=Henry Seile|page=100|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A06468.0001.001
