#*: The Death of Patroclus was the most eminent '''Period'''; and consequently the most proper Time for such Games.
# {{senseid|en|chemistry}}<!--[[d:Q101843]]--> {{lb|en|chemistry}} A [[row]] in the [[periodic table]] of the elements. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{cot|en|group<id:column><!--[[d:Q83306]]-->}}
# {{senseid|en|geology}} {{lb|en|geology}} A [[geochronologic]] [[unit]] of [[million]]s to [[ten]]s of millions of [[year]]s; a [[subdivision]] of an [[era]], and [[subdivided]] into [[epoch]]s.
#: {{ux|en|These fossils are from the Jurassic '''period'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=2017-08-09|author=Mark Carnall|title=Why do cephalopods produce ink? And what's ink made of, anyway?|url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2017/aug/09/why-do-cephalopods-produce-ink-and-what-on-earth-is-it-anyway
|passage=Fossils are particularly well described from the Carboniferous, Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous '''periods''' and have been found in the USA, England, Russia, Lebanon and Germany. Sites such as Lyme Regis in Dorset have particularly yielded number of Jurassic “squid” ink sacs and nodules (Doguzhaeva et al. 2004).|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013195919/https://www.theguardian.com/science/2017/aug/09/why-do-cephalopods-produce-ink-and-what-on-earth-is-it-anyway|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# {{senseid|en|genetics}} {{lb|en|genetics}} A {{m|mul|Drosophila}} [[gene]], the [[gene product]] of which is involved in [[regulation]] of the [[circadian rhythm]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Antibodies to the period gene product of drosophila reveal diverse tissue distribution and rhythmic changes in the visual system|url=http://www.cell.com/neuron/abstract/0896-6273%2888%2990198-5|volume=1|issue=2|page=141|date=1 April 1988|journal=Neuron|passage=Polyclonal antibodies were prepared against the '''''period''''' gene product, which influences biological rhythms in D. melanogaster, by using small synthetic peptides from the per sequence as immunogens.}}
# {{senseid|en|music}} {{lb|en|music}} Two [[phrase]]s (an [[antecedent phrase|antecedent]] and a [[consequent phrase|consequent]] phrase).
# {{senseid|en|math}} {{lb|en|math}} The [[length]] of an [[interval]] over which a [[periodic function]], periodic [[sequence]] or [[repeating decimal]] repeats; often the least such length.

